# Chronos-AEGIS

Chronos-AEGIS: A Cognitive Mission Intelligence (CMI) Framework for
Explainable, Trust-Aware Reasoning over Cyber-Physical Systems.

**Source of truth:** [`paper/Chronos-AEGIS_CMI_Manuscript_v4.pdf`](paper/Chronos-AEGIS_CMI_Manuscript_v4.pdf).
Every module in this repository is checked against that manuscript; see
[`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) for the full section-by-section
map of paper component -> code location -> implementation status.

## Implementation status (Phase 8)

This is a large, multi-phase undertaking (a faithful implementation of a
~20-section research manuscript's full architecture, a Q1-journal-ready
paper package, and a production deployment). Work proceeds as a narrow,
*real* vertical slice at a time rather than stubbing everything shallowly:

**Implemented, tested, and faithful to the manuscript:**
- The Cognitive Mission State sigma(m,t) and Mission Utility U(sigma) (Section 4)
- The Temporal Reasoning Memory Graph (TRMG) and Temporal Dependency Analysis (Section 6)
- The Mission-Aware GNN's attention mechanism and prediction heads, Trust
  Propagation, the Bayesian posterior update, and the Mission Criticality
  Model (Section 7)
- The TTRN pipeline / Algorithm 1 orchestration (Section 5), wired via
  dependency injection against the sections below
- Episodic Mission Memory (Section 8, `memory/`): all four memory tiers,
  Algorithm 2 (Memory Update and Consolidation) and Algorithm 3 (Memory
  Retrieval), including batched consolidation. See `memory/README.md` for
  the documented simplifications (e.g. indexing structure, agent
  calibration metric) that keep it honest about where it diverges from the
  manuscript's reference design.
- Structured AI Argumentation (Section 9, `agents/argumentation/`): the
  Argumentation Graph, Dung-style grounded/preferred extension semantics,
  Conflict(H) and credibility-weighted consensus, and Algorithm 4
  (Structured Argument Aggregation) -- including its escalation-to-human
  path for genuinely unresolvable disagreement. See `agents/README.md` for
  documented simplifications (e.g. preferred-extension tie-break, default
  credibility).
- Causal Mission Intelligence (Section 10, `reasoning/causal/`): a
  Structural Causal Model (domain-supplied, not discovered -- Section
  10.4 states causal discovery is unsolved), the retained correlational
  Counterfactual Engine (Section 10.1), Algorithm 5 (Causal Counterfactual
  Generation) with its explicit causal-vs-correlational labeling, and the
  eight-level Explainability Pipeline (Section 10.7). See
  `reasoning/causal/README.md` for documented scope limitations (e.g. only
  interventional/do queries, not full observational/counterfactual
  abduction).
- The AI Patch Scheduler (Section 14, Algorithm 6, `scheduler/`): the
  feasibility check and weighted-cost argmin over `{patch_now, delay}`.
  Every input Algorithm 1 itself calls (memory, argumentation, causal,
  scheduling) is now real.
- Cognitive Cyber Reasoning, Mission-Aware Cyber Risk Propagation, and the
  Temporal Attack Graph (Sections 11-13, `cyber/`): the shared
  similarity-weighted mission-consequence formula (mu(q)), base risk
  propagation (a widest-path generalization of Trust Propagation),
  AI-driven attack-chain reasoning, supply-chain/firmware trust decay,
  zero-trust reasoning, adversarial AI robustness checks, and all three
  mission-aware cyber metrics -- MTES, MDRI (via a real betweenness-
  centrality implementation), and CMCS. Algorithm 6's CyberExposure term
  is now computed from real MTES values (`cyber/scheduler_integration.py`)
  -- **no placeholder cyber reasoning remains anywhere in the scheduler
  path.** See `cyber/README.md` for the full component map and every
  documented assumption the manuscript left underspecified.
- The Mission Digital Twin (Section 15, `digital_twin/`): a real
  discrete-event simulation engine -- a monotonic clock, a deterministic
  event queue, all twelve named event types, Section 15.1's ten canonical
  scenarios (plus a baseline and two composites), and verified
  deterministic replay. It is now the *producer* Algorithm 1 consumes
  (`digital_twin/integration.py`), not a separate mock: `TTRNPipeline`
  runs against the twin's own real TRMG, unchanged. See
  `digital_twin/README.md`.
- The Evaluation Framework's infrastructure (Section 17, `evaluation/`,
  `scripts/`): batch scenario/seed/parameter sweeps, per-scenario metric
  aggregation with confidence intervals, standard-library statistics
  (Cohen's d, Holm-Bonferroni, and scipy-optional paired significance
  tests), matplotlib-optional visualization, and runnable experiment
  scripts that have actually been executed against real Digital-Twin-
  generated data (not fabricated). See `evaluation/README.md` for the
  real findings.
- Adaptive Learning and Continual Reasoning (Section 16, `reasoning/continual/`):
  Algorithm 7 (MissionAdapt) and the Regularized Continual Objective
  (Section 16.2), with analytic gradients verified against finite
  differences to machine precision. Section 16.4's meta-learning outer
  loop is explicitly out of scope per the manuscript's own text ("a
  design possibility, not a claim"). See `docs/continual-learning-guide.md`
  for two honest findings this implementation surfaced.
- A Benchmark Suite (`benchmarks/`), a real ablation
  (`evaluation/ablation.py`, Section 17.3's No-EMM condition), and a
  one-command reproducibility workflow (`scripts/reproduce_all.py`) that
  regenerates every dataset, runs every benchmark, and exports
  publication-ready tables/figures with a verifiable content hash. See
  `docs/benchmark-guide.md` and `docs/reproducibility-guide.md`.
- **A real, manuscript-consistent fix to Episodic Memory retrieval
  (RQ6/RQ7)**: default retrieval precision@1 was originally *below* its
  own majority-class baseline (0.3846 vs. 0.6154); diagnosed to a genuine
  feature-extraction coverage gap and fixed by reading incoming-edge
  confidence into the similarity function's continuous features -- now
  0.7778 vs. a 0.5000 baseline on the current, expanded scenario library.
  See `memory/README.md` and `evaluation/README.md`.
- **Five new Digital Twin scenarios** (`digital_twin/scenarios.py`'s
  `FIRMWARE_SCENARIO_BUILDERS`) that exercise MDRI's dependency term for
  real, closing a gap Phase 7 discovered (every canonical scenario
  produced `|Dep(m)| = 0`); the original ten canonical scenarios are left
  completely unmodified for before/after comparison.

**Not started:** any of the named RQ baselines Section 17 needs
(RQ2/RQ5/RQ8/RQ9/RQ12 -- see `evaluation/README.md`), the production
backend and frontend, and Render deployment. See each directory's
`README.md` for what it still needs and why it's blocked on the
components above.

No RQ result is reported anywhere in this repository. The manuscript
itself states its evaluation protocol "has not yet been executed" -- this
repository does not get ahead of that. Several small, partial, real
experiments have been run (see `evaluation/README.md`, "What has actually
been run") -- none is a claimed RQ result, and all report findings
exactly as computed, including unfavorable ones where that is what was
measured (the original memory-retrieval regression above; the No-EMM
ablation showing zero effect on any GNN prediction-head metric; the EWC
penalty term's gradient being structurally zero under a literal single-
step-per-mission reading of Algorithm 7).

**A real cross-process determinism bug was found and fixed this phase**:
`digital_twin/twin.py`'s per-node feature seed used Python's built-in
`hash()`, which is salted per-process by `PYTHONHASHSEED` by default --
looking perfectly deterministic within any single process (including an
entire pytest run) while silently producing different results across
separate `python` invocations with the same seed. Found while verifying
`scripts/reproduce_all.py`'s reproducibility claim; fixed via
`hashlib.sha256`. See `docs/reproducibility-guide.md`.

## Repository layout

```
paper/          manuscript (source of truth); Q1 paper package -- not yet built
docs/           architecture map, developer/continual-learning/benchmark/reproducibility guides
reasoning/      CMI state, mission utility, TTRN pipeline, trust layer, causal, continual (Section 16) -- all implemented
graph/          TRMG core data model + Temporal Dependency Analysis + snapshot/pruning (Phase 8)
memory/         Episodic Mission Memory -- implemented (Section 8), + Phase 8 Episodic Learning
agents/         multi-agent hypothesis roster + Structured AI Argumentation -- implemented (Section 9)
cyber/          cognitive cyber reasoning, risk propagation, attack graph, cyber metrics -- implemented (Sections 11-13)
scheduler/      AI Patch Scheduler -- implemented (Section 14), + Phase 8 weight calibration
digital_twin/   Mission Digital Twin -- implemented (Section 15): discrete-event engine, 18-scenario library, replay, dataset export
evaluation/     evaluation infrastructure (Section 17): batch sweeps, aggregation, statistics, ablation; no RQ baseline yet
benchmarks/     self-benchmarking suite (Phase 8): scalability, convergence, retrieval, scheduler quality -- not RQ baselines
datasets/       simulation/evaluation data -- generated/ populated by scripts/, gitignored
backend/        FastAPI production API -- not started
frontend/       React/Next.js dashboard -- not started
deployment/     Render config -- not started
docker/         containerization -- not started
scripts/        experiment scripts + the one-command reproducibility workflow (Phase 8)
configs/        default.yaml -- CMI reasoning parameters
examples/       real, runnable end-to-end demos (incremental + capstone)
tests/          unit/integration tests for everything implemented
```

## Getting started

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest                                                    # 548 tests, all passing (+ 5 honest skips: matplotlib not installed)
python -m examples.run_ttrn_pipeline                      # Algorithm 1, no optional backends
python -m examples.run_ttrn_pipeline_with_memory           # + real EMM across 3 missions
python -m examples.run_ttrn_pipeline_with_argumentation    # + real Structured AI Argumentation
python -m examples.run_ttrn_pipeline_with_causal           # + real causal engine + 8-level explanation
python -m examples.run_ttrn_pipeline_with_scheduler        # + real cyber reasoning + AI Patch Scheduler (full capstone)
python -m scripts.reproduce_all                           # one command: datasets + benchmarks + ablation + tables/figures
python -m scripts.experiment_rq12_cyber_metrics            # Digital Twin + MDRI, canonical vs. firmware scenarios
python -m scripts.experiment_rq6_rq7_memory_retrieval      # Digital Twin + Episodic Memory retrieval precision, before/after
ruff check .                                              # lint
mypy graph reasoning memory agents scheduler cyber digital_twin evaluation benchmarks scripts --ignore-missing-imports  # type-check
```

Optional dependencies -- neither is required, and their absence never
silently degrades a result into a fabricated one (see
`evaluation/README.md` and `digital_twin/visualization.py`):

```bash
pip install scipy         # enables real paired-t-test/Wilcoxon p-values in evaluation.statistics.paired_comparison
pip install matplotlib    # enables digital_twin.visualization's plotting functions
```

